<div class="page-title">
  <h1>Try Elsewhere</h1>
  <p class="lead">The quickest way is solo mode. You play every part yourself: one agent tests the guide, the other compares and suggests fixes. It takes about 5 minutes.</p>
</div>
<article class="notebook">
  <section class="notebook-entry" aria-labelledby="what-you-need">
    <aside class="entry-margin">
      <p class="margin-note">Get ready</p>
    </aside>
    <div class="entry-rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="entry-content">
      <p class="entry-kicker">01 · BEFORE YOU START</p>
      <h2 id="what-you-need">What you need</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>A Switch server you can sign in to with a password.</li>
        <li>OpenCode on the computer that runs the agents.</li>
        <li>A read-only GitHub connection in Switch for the repo the agent should read.</li>
        <li>A copy of this repo: <code class="code--path">git clone https:/<wbr>/<wbr>github.com/<wbr>CryptoZephyr/<wbr>elsewhere.git</code></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="solo" class="notebook-entry" aria-labelledby="solo-heading">
    <aside class="entry-margin">
      <p class="margin-note">Try it yourself</p>
    </aside>
    <div class="entry-rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="entry-content">
      <p class="entry-kicker">02 · SOLO TEST</p>
      <h2 id="solo-heading">Run a solo test</h2>
      <ol class="procedure-steps">
        <li>In the repo folder, run: <code>scripts/elsewhere solo --project my-project --repo OWNER/NAME</code>. Use your GitHub repo for OWNER/NAME.</li>
        <li>Wait for "ready". It takes a few seconds, and the command prints a message for you to copy.</li>
        <li>Paste that message into the new room's first thread in your Switch chat.</li>
        <li>Watch the agents work. If something fails, reply "approve A" or "approve B".</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="plain-note">If your Python is 3.10 or newer, every step passes and you'll get an all-pass report instead of a fix. The real run on the home page shows what a failure looks like.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="notebook-entry" aria-labelledby="with-volunteers">
    <aside class="entry-margin">
      <p class="margin-note">Bring people in</p>
    </aside>
    <div class="entry-rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="entry-content">
      <p class="entry-kicker">03 · WITH REAL VOLUNTEERS</p>
      <h2 id="with-volunteers">Test with other people</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>You run: <code>scripts/elsewhere maintainer --project my-project --repo OWNER/NAME</code>. It prints a join code.</li>
        <li>Each volunteer runs on their own computer: <code>scripts/elsewhere volunteer --join JOIN_CODE --handle volunteer-1</code>.</li>
        <li>Paste the printed message into the room's first thread, and @mention your agent whenever you reply.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="notebook-entry" aria-labelledby="more-options">
    <aside class="entry-margin">
      <p class="margin-note">More detail</p>
    </aside>
    <div class="entry-rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="entry-content">
      <p class="entry-kicker">04 · MORE OPTIONS</p>
      <h2 id="more-options">More options</h2>

      <details>
        <summary>Connecting to your Switch server</summary>
        <div class="details-content">
          <p>The default Gateway is the local HTTPS server.</p>
          <dl class="leader-list">
            <div class="leader-row">
              <dt><span class="leader-label"><code>SWITCH_GATEWAY_API</code></span><span class="leader-fill" aria-hidden="true"></span></dt>
              <dd>Set <code>SWITCH_GATEWAY_API</code> when the server isn't at that address. A Gateway URL must end in <code>/gateway</code>. Remote URLs must use HTTPS.</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="leader-row">
              <dt><span class="leader-label"><code>SWITCH_GATEWAY_EMAIL</code></span><span class="leader-fill" aria-hidden="true"></span></dt>
              <dd>Set <code>SWITCH_GATEWAY_EMAIL</code> to skip the email prompt.</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="leader-row">
              <dt><span class="leader-label"><code>SWITCH_GATEWAY_PASSWORD</code></span><span class="leader-fill" aria-hidden="true"></span></dt>
              <dd>Set <code>SWITCH_GATEWAY_PASSWORD</code> in the environment or enter it at the hidden prompt. The password and session cookie aren't written to disk.</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="leader-row">
              <dt><span class="leader-label"><code>SWITCH_CA_FILE</code> / <code class="code--flag">--ca-file PATH</code></span><span class="leader-fill" aria-hidden="true"></span></dt>
              <dd>For a private or self-signed Gateway, set <code>SWITCH_CA_FILE</code> or pass <code class="code--flag">--ca-file PATH</code> with a trusted CA certificate. TLS verification stays on.</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Command options</summary>
        <div class="details-content">
          <p>During real setup, the maintainer command uses <code>opencode/big-pickle</code> by default.</p>
          <dl class="leader-list">
            <div class="leader-row">
              <dt><span class="leader-label"><code class="code--flag">--model MODEL</code></span><span class="leader-fill" aria-hidden="true"></span></dt>
              <dd>Pass <code class="code--flag">--model MODEL</code> to select another model.</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="leader-row">
              <dt><span class="leader-label"><code class="code--flag">--machine NAME</code></span><span class="leader-fill" aria-hidden="true"></span></dt>
              <dd>Use <code class="code--flag">--machine NAME</code> to choose a controller.</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="leader-row">
              <dt><span class="leader-label"><code class="code--flag">--dry-run</code></span><span class="leader-fill" aria-hidden="true"></span></dt>
              <dd>Add <code class="code--flag">--dry-run</code> to any command to print planned requests without calling the API or changing workdirs.</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="leader-row">
              <dt><span class="leader-label"><code class="code--flag">--machine NAME</code> / <code class="code--flag">--model MODEL</code></span><span class="leader-fill" aria-hidden="true"></span></dt>
              <dd>If needed, add <code class="code--flag">--machine NAME</code> to select a controller and <code class="code--flag">--model MODEL</code> to choose a model.</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="leader-row">
              <dt><span class="leader-label"><code class="code--flag">--machine NAME</code></span><span class="leader-fill" aria-hidden="true"></span></dt>
              <dd>Volunteers can add <code class="code--flag">--machine NAME</code> to select a controller.</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
          <p>The maintainer workdir is separate from the project checkout and must contain only <code>opencode.json</code>.</p>
          <p>The maintainer deploys the room and its agent. The command prints a room name, an invite line, and a request message for the kickoff thread.</p>
          <p>Each volunteer runs one command on their own machine with a unique lowercase handle.</p>
          <p>The command clones this public repo into the empty workdir before any session, checks the effective OpenCode permissions, and confirms there isn't an existing session. It then adds only the maintainer agent and linked messaging identity to the room's addressing policy.</p>
          <p>The join code contains the project slug and Switch IDs. It isn't an access credential. A volunteer must sign in to an account that can access the room.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Rules for the chat thread</summary>
        <div class="details-content">
          <p>After everyone is ready, paste the printed request into the kickoff thread. Keep the exact wording and always @mention the maintainer. Keep reports, clarifications, approvals, and rerun results in that thread. Mention the maintainer in every human reply that asks a question or approves an option.</p>
          <p>The printed request names <code>sample/QUICKSTART.md</code> and <code>sample/hello.py</code>. The maintainer may read those files without asking and must ask before reading any other source path. Volunteers send every requested result to the maintainer with a targeted message and also post it in the thread.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>If something goes wrong</summary>
        <div class="details-content troubleshooting">
          <h3>Certificate errors</h3>
          <dl class="leader-list">
            <div class="leader-row">
              <dt><span class="leader-label">Certificate</span><span class="leader-fill" aria-hidden="true"></span></dt>
              <dd>Set <code>SWITCH_CA_FILE</code> or pass <code class="code--flag">--ca-file PATH</code> with the trusted CA certificate. Don't turn off TLS verification.</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
          <h3>A reply didn't reach the maintainer</h3>
          <p>Replies without an @mention may not be delivered to the agent. Mention the maintainer in each approval or clarification. Volunteers should send results with a targeted message as well as posting in the thread.</p>
          <h3>A session started before config was ready</h3>
          <p>Don't use a session that started before the workdir config was placed. Redeploy with a new project slug, then post the kickoff request after setup is ready.</p>
          <p><a href="how-it-works.html">Read how the workflow works.</a></p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
</article>
